## Why This Is Asked
The question probes practical prompt governance: how to prove provenance and detect tampering from model transformations in a multi-tenant setting. It tests attack awareness, deterministic auditing, and safe-guard design under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Watermarking robustness under paraphrase/translation
- Non-semantic embedding and tamper detection
- Audit logging for per-tenant prompts
- Edge-case handling and false positives

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal watermark prototype (JS)
function addWatermark(prompt, tag) {
  return prompt + " [WM:" + tag + "]";
}
function detectWatermark(text) {
  const m = text.match(/\[WM:([A-Za-z0-9._-]+)\]/);
  return m ? m[1] : null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate watermark robustness across languages/models?
- How would you scale to thousands of tenants with dynamic watermark policies?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design per-tenant policy enforcement, guard-detection, and prompt rewriting with auditable provenance.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant policy registry and per-tenant constraints
- Guard-detection vs. prompt rewriting
- System guard injection and explicit privacy constraints
- Audit artifacts and drift/telemetry considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
const policy = {
  Tesla: { pii: true, retentionDays: 90 },
  Google: { pii: false, retentionDays: 365 },
  Plaid: { pii: true, retentionDays: 30 }
};
function guardAndRewrite(tenant, prompt) {
  const violations = [];
  const lower = prompt.toLowerCase();
  if (lower.includes("password") || lower.includes("ssn")) {
    violations.push("PII-like content");
  }
  const rewritten = `[${tenant}] ${prompt} /* guard: ensure policy compliance */`;
  return { rewritten, violations };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evaluate determinism and drift for two tenants under evolving policies?  
- What tests would you add to catch policy-evading prompts without compromising latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a scalable, secure prompt pipeline that enforces per-tenant policies, blocks prompt-injection, and records privacy-preserving audits in real time.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy schemas (allowed_actions, data_classifications)
- Safety shims against system-prompt leakage and prompt-injection
- Privacy-preserving reasoning traces in audit logs
- Latency budgeting and streaming guarantees

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype components for the three stages
function validatePolicy(prompt, tenantPolicy) {
  // check allowed_actions and data_classifications
  const okAction = tenantPolicy.allowed_actions.includes('execute') || prompt.includes('export');
  const safeData = !/SECRET|PASSWORD/i.test(prompt);
  return okAction && safeData;
}

function guardPrompt(prompt, policy) {
  // simple sanitizer to remove system prompts or injected cues
  let p = prompt.replace(/system prompt:.*$/i, '').trim();
  return p;
}

function logAudit(input, modelVersion, latency, reasoning) {
  return {
    input: input, modelVersion, latency, reasoningTrace: reasoning
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this against non-determinism and ensure consistent audits at scale?
- What metrics would you collect to detect policy drift or leakage risk over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests architecture for intent resolution, safe routing, and token budgeting under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Intent detection and confidence thresholds
- Template routing to data-extraction, summarization, code-generation
- Safety guards to prevent system-prompt leakage
- Token budgeting and edge-case handling

## Code Example
```typescript
type Template = 'data-extraction'|'summarization'|'code-generation';
type Payload = { template: Template; tokenBudget: number; justification: string; };
function routePrompt(prompt: string, signals: {intent?: string; confidence?: number;}): Payload {
  const t = (signals.intent === 'summarize' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'summarization'
            : (signals.intent === 'extract' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'data-extraction'
            : (signals.intent === 'generate' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'code-generation'
            : 'summarization';
  const budgets: Record<Template, number> = {'data-extraction': 800, 'summarization': 500, 'code-generation': 1200};
  return { template: t, tokenBudget: budgets[t], justification: 'inferred from signals with fallback' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to n templates and multilingual prompts?
- How would you test guardrail effectiveness under prompt injections?
